/* CSO Cyber Range — Course content: BC-105 Introduction to the Risk Management Framework (RMF) */
window.COURSE_DATA = window.COURSE_DATA || {};
window.COURSE_DATA["BC-105"] = {
ov:"The Risk Management Framework is the structured process by which federal and DoD systems are secured and authorized to operate. This course walks the framework's foundations and its opening steps — Prepare, Categorize, Select — where most of the consequential decisions are made. You will learn to categorize a system defensibly and tailor a control baseline to fit it. Each module ends in a required knowledge check; the 40-item capstone draws two items per module plus applied scenarios.",
mods:[
{t:"RMF Origins and Legal Mandates",d:"FISMA, OMB, and why the framework exists.",
obj:["Explain the legal basis of the RMF in FISMA and related authorities","Identify the roles of NIST, OMB, and agencies","Explain the shift from certification-and-accreditation to continuous risk management"],
read:["The RMF exists because law requires federal information systems to be secured and formally authorized. The Federal Information Security Modernization Act (FISMA) mandates that agencies protect their information and systems commensurate with risk, directs NIST to develop the standards and guidelines, and gives OMB oversight and enforcement authority. This chain — statute to NIST standards to agency implementation — is why the framework is not optional guidance but a compliance obligation with real consequences for the systems it governs.",
"NIST translates the mandate into the framework and its supporting catalog: SP 800-37 defines the RMF process, FIPS 199 and SP 800-60 govern categorization, FIPS 200 and SP 800-53 define controls, and a family of special publications fills in the details. The DoD implements all of this through DoDI 8510.01 and tools like eMASS. Understanding this document ecosystem matters because RMF work constantly references these publications by number, and knowing which document governs which decision is basic professional fluency.",
"The RMF replaced the older Certification and Accreditation model, and the shift is philosophical, not just procedural. Where the old model treated authorization as a periodic, point-in-time event, the RMF frames security as continuous risk management: systems are authorized based on assessed risk and then monitored continuously, with authorization maintained through ongoing assessment rather than expiring and being redone from scratch. This ongoing-risk framing (which BC-101 introduced as resilience thinking) is the intellectual core of the whole framework and explains why the seventh step, Monitor, never ends."],
ex:"An agency system owner asks why they cannot simply install good security tools and skip the paperwork. The answer is legal: FISMA requires the system be categorized, controlled per NIST standards, assessed, and formally authorized by an accountable official before it may operate on the network — and audited for it. The RMF is the mandated process that converts security work into a defensible, accountable risk decision.",
refs:[["FISMA (44 U.S.C. § 3551 et seq.)","The statutory basis requiring federal information security and authorization."],["NIST SP 800-37 Rev.2, Ch.1","The RMF's purpose, authorities, and relationship to FISMA and other publications."]],
kc:[
{q:"The legal foundation requiring federal systems to be secured and authorized is:",o:["The RMF itself","FISMA, which mandates protection commensurate with risk and directs NIST to set standards","A vendor contract","ISO 27001"],a:1,x:"FISMA is the statute; NIST develops the standards and OMB oversees — the RMF is how agencies implement the legal mandate."},
{q:"The RMF differs from the older Certification and Accreditation model chiefly by:",o:["Requiring less documentation","Treating authorization as continuous risk management with ongoing monitoring rather than a point-in-time event","Eliminating authorization","Removing NIST from the process"],a:1,x:"The RMF frames security as continuous — authorize on assessed risk, then monitor continuously — replacing periodic re-accreditation from scratch."},
{q:"Which organization develops the standards and guidelines the RMF is built on?",o:["OMB","NIST","The vendor community","The GAO"],a:1,x:"FISMA directs NIST to develop the standards (FIPS) and guidelines (SP 800 series) that define the framework; OMB provides oversight."}]},
{t:"The Seven RMF Steps",d:"Prepare, Categorize, Select, Implement, Assess, Authorize, Monitor.",
obj:["Name the seven RMF steps in order and state each purpose","Explain how the steps produce an authorization decision","Identify the key output of each step"],
read:["The RMF is seven steps executed in sequence, though with feedback loops. Prepare establishes context — roles, risk tolerance, and a system-level foundation. Categorize determines the system's impact level based on the information it handles. Select chooses and tailors the control baseline to protect it. Implement puts the controls in place and documents how. Assess independently tests whether the controls work as intended. Authorize is the accountable official's formal risk-acceptance decision. Monitor maintains the authorization through continuous assessment. Each step produces a defined output that feeds the next.",
"The steps build toward a single decision: whether a senior official will accept the residual risk of operating the system. Categorize sets the protection bar; Select and Implement raise the system to that bar; Assess measures how well; and the resulting package — system security plan, assessment report, and plan of action and milestones — is what the Authorizing Official weighs to grant or deny an Authorization to Operate. Understanding this arc keeps the individual steps from feeling like disconnected bureaucracy; each exists to inform or execute that risk decision.",
"This course covers Prepare, Categorize, and Select in depth because they front-load the consequential decisions — miscategorize a system and every downstream control is wrong; select the wrong baseline and you over- or under-protect. Implement, Assess, Authorize, and Monitor are developed in the operational tier (IC-204 for eMASS execution, BC-107 for assessment). Knowing the whole arc now lets you place each detailed topic and understand why the early steps deserve the most careful attention."],
ex:"A new system's RMF effort begins. The team runs Prepare (assigning roles and confirming risk tolerance), then Categorize (determining it is a Moderate-impact system), then Select (tailoring the Moderate baseline). Only after these does implementation begin. Because categorization drove baseline selection, getting it right early meant the controls fit the system's actual risk — the leverage of the opening steps in action.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-37 Rev.2, Ch.3","The seven RMF steps, their tasks, and their outputs."],["NIST RMF Quick Start Guides","Concise per-step overviews and roles."]],
kc:[
{q:"The correct order of the RMF steps is:",o:["Categorize, Select, Prepare, Assess, Implement, Monitor, Authorize","Prepare, Categorize, Select, Implement, Assess, Authorize, Monitor","Select, Prepare, Categorize, Authorize, Assess, Implement, Monitor","Prepare, Select, Categorize, Implement, Authorize, Assess, Monitor"],a:1,x:"Prepare, Categorize, Select, Implement, Assess, Authorize, Monitor — categorization precedes selection, and monitoring is continuous."},
{q:"All seven RMF steps ultimately serve to produce:",o:["A vendor purchase order","A senior official's formal risk-acceptance decision (the authorization)","A network diagram","A training certificate"],a:1,x:"The framework converts security work into the package on which the Authorizing Official accepts residual risk — the ATO."},
{q:"Why does categorization deserve especially careful attention?",o:["It is the last step","It sets the protection bar; miscategorization makes every downstream control selection wrong","It is optional","It only affects documentation"],a:1,x:"Categorization drives baseline selection, so an error there propagates into over- or under-protecting the entire system."}]},
{t:"Step 1: Prepare — Organization Level",d:"Context, roles, and risk strategy before any system.",
obj:["Describe the organization-level Prepare tasks","Explain the role of the risk management strategy and risk tolerance","Explain common controls and their organizational value"],
read:["The Prepare step, added prominently in RMF Rev.2, establishes the context that makes everything else coherent, and it operates at two levels. At the organization level, leadership defines the risk management strategy — how much risk the organization is willing to accept (its risk tolerance), how risk is assessed and communicated, and who holds which roles. This top-down framing means individual system decisions are made against a consistent standard rather than each team inventing its own risk appetite.",
"A central organization-level output is the identification of common controls: security controls provided once at the organizational or infrastructure level and inherited by many systems (physical security of the data center, network boundary protection, enterprise identity services). Common controls are enormously efficient — implement and assess once, inherit many times, echoing the single-control-inventory principle from BC-101 — and they let system teams focus on the controls unique to their system rather than re-implementing enterprise services. Getting the common-control catalog right is a high-leverage organizational investment.",
"Organizational Prepare also establishes the enterprise architecture and security architecture context, continuous monitoring strategy, and the assignment of key roles (Risk Executive, AO, ISSM). This groundwork is why Prepare precedes everything: a system-level RMF effort launched without organizational risk tolerance, a common-control catalog, or clear roles ends up making inconsistent, undefendable decisions. The step embodies the governance discipline of BC-101 — decide the framing once, at the top, so every system decision inherits it."],
ex:"An agency defines its risk tolerance as low for internet-facing systems handling PII and establishes enterprise common controls for boundary protection and identity. A new system team inherits these common controls and applies the low-risk-tolerance framing to their decisions — rather than each team guessing at acceptable risk and rebuilding boundary defenses, they inherit a consistent standard and shared services from organizational Prepare.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-37 Rev.2, Section 3.1 (Prepare)","Organization-level Prepare tasks including risk strategy and common controls."],["NIST SP 800-39","Managing information security risk at the organization, mission, and system levels."]],
kc:[
{q:"A primary organization-level output of the Prepare step is:",o:["The final authorization","The risk management strategy/risk tolerance and the catalog of common controls","The assessment report","The penetration test"],a:1,x:"Organizational Prepare sets consistent risk framing and identifies inheritable common controls before any system-level work begins."},
{q:"Common controls provide efficiency because they are:",o:["Implemented separately by each system","Implemented and assessed once at the organizational level and inherited by many systems","Never assessed","Unique to one system"],a:1,x:"Enterprise-provided controls (data-center physical security, boundary protection) are inherited, avoiding redundant per-system implementation."},
{q:"Why must organization-level Prepare precede system-level work?",o:["It is traditional","So system decisions inherit consistent risk tolerance, roles, and common controls rather than each team inventing its own","It is faster","It replaces categorization"],a:1,x:"Without shared risk framing and roles, individual systems make inconsistent, undefendable decisions — Prepare sets the common foundation."}]},
{t:"Step 1: Prepare — System Level",d:"Boundaries, stakeholders, and the system's risk context.",
obj:["Define the authorization boundary and why it matters","Identify system-level Prepare tasks and stakeholders","Explain how the mission and data drive the system's risk context"],
read:["System-level Prepare establishes the specific context for one system, and its most consequential product is the authorization boundary — the definition of what is and is not part of the system being authorized. The boundary determines what the controls protect, what the assessment examines, and what the authorization covers. Drawing it too broadly makes the effort unmanageable; too narrowly leaves connected components unauthorized and creates the undocumented-interconnection problem BC-101 flagged. A clear, defensible boundary is foundational to everything that follows.",
"System Prepare also identifies stakeholders (owner, ISSM, ISSO, users, and the AO), the mission the system supports, the information it processes, and its interconnections and dependencies. This context is what makes categorization meaningful in the next step — you cannot assign impact levels without knowing what data the system holds and what mission it enables. It also surfaces inherited common controls and the specific controls the system must provide itself, setting up the Select step.",
"The discipline here is to define the system accurately before protecting it, because every later step assumes an accurate boundary and context. A system whose boundary omits a database it depends on, or whose data inventory misses a category of PII, will be mis-categorized and mis-controlled no matter how well the later steps are executed. Prepare is unglamorous scoping work, but as the earlier courses repeatedly showed, decisions made from an inaccurate picture of the system fail regardless of downstream effort."],
ex:"A team defines a web application's authorization boundary to include its app servers, database, and load balancer, but documents that it inherits the enterprise's boundary firewall and identity service as common controls. This precise boundary means the assessment examines the right components and the authorization covers exactly the system — avoiding both an unmanageably broad scope and the gap of an undocumented dependency.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-37 Rev.2, Section 3.1 (system-level Prepare)","System-level tasks including boundary, stakeholders, and context."],["NIST SP 800-18","Guide for developing system security plans, including boundary definition."]],
kc:[
{q:"The authorization boundary defines:",o:["The network's IP range only","What is and is not part of the system being authorized — what the controls protect and the authorization covers","The budget","The number of users"],a:1,x:"The boundary scopes the entire effort: controls, assessment, and authorization all apply to exactly what the boundary includes."},
{q:"A boundary drawn too narrowly, omitting a database the system depends on, causes:",o:["Faster authorization","An unauthorized, unassessed dependency — the undocumented-interconnection problem","Better security","Lower cost with no risk"],a:1,x:"Excluded-but-relied-upon components fall outside the controls and authorization, leaving a gap that undermines the risk decision."},
{q:"System-level Prepare must accurately capture the data and mission because:",o:["It is required for billing","Categorization and control selection depend on knowing what data the system holds and what mission it enables","It speeds up monitoring","It replaces the SSP"],a:1,x:"Impact levels and controls flow from the system's data and mission; an inaccurate picture mis-categorizes and mis-protects the system."}]},
{t:"Risk Tolerance and the Risk Executive",d:"Enterprise risk framing and consistent decisions.",
obj:["Define risk tolerance and its role in RMF decisions","Explain the Risk Executive function","Explain how enterprise risk framing keeps system decisions consistent"],
read:["Risk tolerance is the amount and type of risk an organization is willing to accept in pursuit of its mission, and it is the yardstick against which every authorization decision is measured. Without an explicit risk tolerance, one Authorizing Official might accept a residual risk that another would reject, producing inconsistent protection across the enterprise. Setting risk tolerance at the organizational level (during Prepare) means that when an AO weighs a system's residual risk, they weigh it against a defined standard, not personal instinct.",
"The Risk Executive (function) provides this enterprise-wide consistency. It is a role — sometimes an individual, often a group — responsible for ensuring that risk decisions across all systems align with organizational risk tolerance and strategy, that risk is managed holistically rather than system-by-system in isolation, and that risks affecting multiple systems or the whole mission are considered above the level of any single AO. This addresses a real failure mode: individually acceptable system risks that, in aggregate, create unacceptable enterprise risk.",
"The practical effect is that RMF decisions are not made in a vacuum. When a system team documents a residual risk and an AO considers accepting it, both operate within the risk tolerance and strategy the organization set, informed by the Risk Executive's enterprise view. This connects to the governance and accountability lessons of BC-101 — risk acceptance is a serious, accountable act bounded by organizational policy, not a discretionary local choice. It is why the same residual risk may be acceptable in one organization and not another."],
ex:"A system team proposes accepting the risk of a legacy component. Individually the risk seems minor, but the Risk Executive notes that a dozen systems have accepted similar legacy risks, creating an aggregate enterprise exposure that exceeds the organization's risk tolerance. The enterprise view catches what any single AO, seeing only their own system, would have missed — the reason the Risk Executive function exists.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-39, Ch.2-3","Organizational risk framing, risk tolerance, and the risk executive function."],["NIST SP 800-37 Rev.2, roles appendix","The Risk Executive (function) role and responsibilities."]],
kc:[
{q:"Risk tolerance serves as:",o:["A technical control","The organizational yardstick against which authorization decisions weigh residual risk, keeping them consistent","A type of firewall","An assessment method"],a:1,x:"Defined risk tolerance means AOs weigh residual risk against a common standard rather than inconsistent personal judgment."},
{q:"The Risk Executive (function) primarily ensures that:",o:["Each system is authorized in isolation","Risk decisions across all systems align with enterprise risk tolerance and aggregate risks are considered","Passwords are strong","Controls are implemented"],a:1,x:"It provides the enterprise view, catching aggregate and cross-system risks that individual AOs, focused on one system, would miss."},
{q:"Why can the same residual risk be acceptable in one organization but not another?",o:["Different firewalls","Because organizations set different risk tolerances against which the risk is weighed","Different programming languages","Random chance"],a:1,x:"Acceptability is judged against organizational risk tolerance, which varies — so identical risk yields different decisions in different organizations."}]},
{t:"Step 2: Categorize — FIPS 199",d:"Impact levels for confidentiality, integrity, and availability.",
obj:["Explain the FIPS 199 categorization method","Apply the high-water mark principle","Justify impact-level assignments"],
read:["Categorization determines how much protection a system needs by assessing the potential impact of a security breach, and FIPS 199 defines the method. For each of the three security objectives from BC-101 — confidentiality, integrity, and availability — the system is assigned a potential impact of LOW, MODERATE, or HIGH, reflecting the effect a breach of that objective would have on the organization and its mission. Low means limited adverse effect; Moderate serious; High severe or catastrophic. These are mission-impact judgments, not technical ratings.",
"The overall system categorization uses the high-water mark: the system's impact level is the highest of its three objective ratings. A system rated Low confidentiality, Moderate integrity, and Low availability is a Moderate system overall, because the highest single impact drives the protection requirement. This conservative rule ensures the baseline protects against the worst plausible consequence rather than an average. The result — a triple like {Confidentiality: Moderate, Integrity: Moderate, Availability: Low} plus an overall High-water-mark level — is the input to baseline selection.",
"Categorization is consequential and must be justified, not guessed. Over-categorizing wastes resources protecting a system beyond its mission need and can make it unusably locked down; under-categorizing leaves it dangerously exposed and is a common audit finding. The rating for each objective should trace to a documented rationale about mission impact — what actually happens to the mission if this data is disclosed, corrupted, or unavailable. This is the property-by-property mission analysis BC-101 introduced, now formalized as the pivotal RMF decision that drives everything downstream."],
ex:"A team categorizes a public-affairs website: confidentiality Low (content is public), integrity Moderate (defacement or false information would seriously embarrass and mislead), availability Low (brief outages are tolerable). By the high-water mark the system is Moderate overall, driven by the integrity concern — a defensible rating traceable to mission impact, which then selects the Moderate control baseline.",
refs:[["FIPS 199","The standard defining security categorization and impact levels."],["NIST SP 800-60 Vol.1","Guide to mapping information types to impact levels (module seven)."]],
kc:[
{q:"Under FIPS 199, a system rated Low confidentiality, Moderate integrity, and Low availability is categorized overall as:",o:["Low","Moderate","High","It cannot be categorized"],a:1,x:"The high-water mark takes the highest of the three objective ratings; Moderate integrity makes the system Moderate overall."},
{q:"FIPS 199 impact levels (Low/Moderate/High) measure:",o:["The number of vulnerabilities","The potential adverse effect on the organization and mission if the objective is breached","Network speed","The cost of the system"],a:1,x:"Categorization is a mission-impact judgment about the consequence of a confidentiality, integrity, or availability breach — not a technical metric."},
{q:"Under-categorizing a system is a problem because it:",o:["Wastes money","Selects an insufficient control baseline, leaving the system dangerously under-protected","Makes the system too secure","Speeds up authorization safely"],a:1,x:"Too low a category yields too weak a baseline for the real mission impact — a common and serious audit finding."}]},
{t:"Information Types and SP 800-60",d:"Identifying data and mapping it to impact.",
obj:["Explain how information types drive categorization","Describe using SP 800-60 to map data to provisional impacts","Explain adjusting provisional impacts to context"],
read:["Categorization starts with cataloging the information types a system processes, stores, or transmits — because impact flows from data. NIST SP 800-60 provides a catalog of common information types (from privacy data to financial records to mission-specific categories) each with recommended provisional impact levels for confidentiality, integrity, and availability. The process is to inventory the system's data, map each type to its SP 800-60 entry to get provisional impacts, and then take the high-water mark across all types and objectives.",
"The provisional impacts are a starting point, not a verdict. SP 800-60 explicitly expects the categorizer to adjust based on the specific mission and context: a data type's default impact might be Moderate, but in a particular system its compromise could have High mission consequences (or, occasionally, lower). These adjustments must be documented with rationale, because they are the judgment that tailors a generic catalog to a specific system. Skipping the adjustment and accepting provisional values blindly is a common shortcut that produces mis-categorized systems.",
"This module operationalizes the previous one: FIPS 199 defines the levels, SP 800-60 provides the data-to-impact mapping, and together they produce a defensible categorization traceable to the actual information the system handles. The rigor matters because categorization drives baseline selection and thus the entire control effort. A categorization built from a real data inventory and documented adjustments withstands audit; one built from a guess at the system's overall sensitivity does not, and the difference is exactly this information-type analysis."],
ex:"A financial reporting system inventories its data types — internal financial data, PII, and audit records — and maps each via SP 800-60. Provisional confidentiality is Moderate, but the team documents an adjustment to High because unauthorized disclosure of the pre-release financial figures could move markets, a mission-specific consequence beyond the generic default. The documented adjustment produces a categorization that survives scrutiny.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-60 Vol.1 & Vol.2","Method and catalog for mapping information types to impact levels."],["FIPS 199","The impact-level definitions the mapping produces."]],
kc:[
{q:"Categorization begins with:",o:["Selecting controls","Cataloging the information types the system processes, since impact flows from data","Running a scan","Signing the ATO"],a:1,x:"Impact derives from the data; you inventory information types and map them to impacts before determining the system category."},
{q:"The provisional impact levels in SP 800-60 should be:",o:["Accepted exactly as written always","Adjusted with documented rationale to reflect the specific system's mission and context","Ignored entirely","Averaged together"],a:1,x:"Provisional values are a starting point; SP 800-60 expects context-based adjustment, documented — blindly accepting defaults mis-categorizes systems."},
{q:"A documented adjustment raising a data type's confidentiality from Moderate to High is justified when:",o:["The team prefers High","Disclosure would have High mission-specific consequences beyond the generic default (e.g., market-moving data)","The system is new","It saves money"],a:1,x:"Context-specific mission impact can exceed the catalog default; the adjustment must be justified by that mission consequence."}]},
{t:"Step 3: Select — Control Baselines",d:"FIPS 200, SP 800-53B, and starting points by impact.",
obj:["Explain how categorization selects a control baseline","Describe the Low, Moderate, and High baselines in SP 800-53B","Explain the minimum-security-requirements role of FIPS 200"],
read:["The Select step turns the categorization into a concrete starting set of controls. FIPS 200 establishes the minimum security requirements — mandating that systems implement controls from the SP 800-53 catalog appropriate to their impact level — and SP 800-53B provides the actual baselines: predefined sets of controls for Low, Moderate, and High impact systems. A Moderate system starts from the Moderate baseline, which specifies exactly which controls and enhancements from the catalog apply as a floor.",
"The baselines are cumulative in rigor: the Moderate baseline includes the Low controls plus additional controls and enhancements, and the High baseline adds still more. This reflects the principle that higher-impact systems warrant stronger, more numerous safeguards. The baseline is not the final control set — it is the vetted starting point that the tailoring process (next module) then adjusts to the specific system. Starting from the right baseline for the categorization is why getting categorization correct matters so much: the baseline is chosen directly by the high-water-mark impact level.",
"There is also a privacy dimension: SP 800-53B includes a privacy baseline, and systems processing personally identifiable information apply privacy controls alongside security controls. The Select step thus produces the initial control set — security baseline by impact plus applicable privacy controls — that the system must implement and later be assessed against. This is where the abstract categorization becomes a concrete, if still un-tailored, list of protective requirements, connecting the mission-impact judgment to the specific safeguards of the next course, BC-106."],
ex:"A system categorized Moderate overall selects the SP 800-53B Moderate baseline as its starting control set, and because it processes PII, it also applies the privacy control baseline. The categorization decision made two modules earlier directly determined this starting point — a Moderate categorization pulls the Moderate baseline, neither the lighter Low nor the heavier High.",
refs:[["FIPS 200","Minimum security requirements and the mandate to apply 800-53 baselines by impact."],["NIST SP 800-53B","The Low, Moderate, High, and privacy control baselines."]],
kc:[
{q:"The control baseline a system starts from is determined by:",o:["The budget","The system's FIPS 199 high-water-mark impact level","The number of users","The vendor"],a:1,x:"Categorization selects the baseline: a Moderate system starts from the Moderate baseline in SP 800-53B — hence categorization's leverage."},
{q:"The relationship between the Low, Moderate, and High baselines is that they are:",o:["Completely separate with no overlap","Cumulative — Moderate includes Low plus more; High includes still more","Identical","Chosen randomly"],a:1,x:"Higher-impact baselines add controls and enhancements on top of lower ones, reflecting stronger safeguards for higher consequence."},
{q:"FIPS 200's role in the Select step is to:",o:["Assess controls","Establish minimum security requirements, mandating application of the appropriate 800-53 baseline","Categorize data","Authorize the system"],a:1,x:"FIPS 200 sets the floor — systems must implement the impact-appropriate 800-53 baseline as a minimum."}]},
{t:"Control Tailoring",d:"Adjusting the baseline to fit the system.",
obj:["Define tailoring and its purpose","Describe the tailoring actions: scoping, compensating, parameter assignment","Explain documenting tailoring decisions"],
read:["A baseline is a starting point, not a final answer, and tailoring is the process of adjusting it to fit the specific system while maintaining adequate protection. Tailoring includes identifying and designating common controls (inherited from the organization), applying scoping guidance (removing controls that genuinely do not apply — a wireless control on a system with no wireless), selecting compensating controls where a baseline control is infeasible (BC-101's compensating-control concept), and assigning organization-defined parameters (the baseline says configure a session timeout; tailoring sets it to fifteen minutes).",
"Tailoring is disciplined, not discretionary. Removing a control requires a documented rationale that the risk is genuinely absent or addressed otherwise — you cannot tailor out a control merely because it is inconvenient. Every tailoring action becomes part of the system security plan and is subject to assessment and the AO's scrutiny; an unjustified removal is exactly the kind of finding that undermines an authorization. The goal is a control set that precisely matches the system's real risk — neither the generic baseline applied blindly nor a baseline gutted for convenience.",
"Parameter assignment is where tailoring gets concrete and often overlooked. Many controls contain organization-defined values — password length, lockout thresholds, backup frequency, review cadences — and the baseline leaves these to be set. Setting them thoughtfully (informed by risk tolerance and the categorization) is real security work, and setting them carelessly (accepting weak defaults) undermines otherwise-selected controls. Tailoring, done well, produces the defensible, system-specific control set that the implementation and assessment steps then act on — the bridge from framework to reality."],
ex:"A Moderate system's baseline includes controls for wireless access, but the system has no wireless capability. The team tailors these out with a documented rationale (no wireless present), designates the enterprise firewall as an inherited common control, and sets the organization-defined session-timeout parameter to fifteen minutes per risk tolerance. Each decision is documented in the SSP, producing a control set that fits the system and withstands assessment.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-53B, tailoring guidance","Scoping, compensating controls, and parameter assignment."],["NIST SP 800-37 Rev.2, Select step tasks","How tailoring fits the RMF and its documentation requirements."]],
kc:[
{q:"Tailoring the baseline means:",o:["Adding random controls","Adjusting the baseline to the specific system — scoping, compensating controls, parameters — while maintaining adequate protection","Removing all controls","Ignoring the baseline"],a:1,x:"Tailoring fits the vetted baseline to the actual system through documented, disciplined adjustments, not arbitrary changes."},
{q:"Removing a baseline control during tailoring requires:",o:["No justification","A documented rationale that the risk is genuinely absent or addressed by other means","AO approval only after the audit","Removing it silently"],a:1,x:"Scoping-out a control must be justified; an unjustified removal is a finding that undermines the authorization."},
{q:"Assigning organization-defined parameters (e.g., session timeout value) matters because:",o:["It is optional busywork","Careless defaults undermine otherwise-selected controls; thoughtful values are real security work","Parameters are always fixed","It replaces categorization"],a:1,x:"Many controls leave concrete values to be set; weak defaults gut the control, so parameter assignment is substantive security work."}]},
{t:"Overlays and Control Inheritance",d:"Specialized control sets and shared responsibility.",
obj:["Explain what overlays are and when to use them","Describe control inheritance and shared responsibility","Explain the security implications of inheritance in cloud environments"],
read:["Overlays are pre-built, specialized sets of tailoring decisions for a particular community, technology, or environment — for example, overlays for national security systems, privacy, cloud, or specific mission types. Rather than each team independently tailoring for a common context, an overlay encodes the expert consensus for that context, applied on top of the baseline. Using an applicable overlay is both efficient and more defensible than reinventing the tailoring, much as security baselines beat ad hoc hardening in BC-103.",
"Control inheritance formalizes shared responsibility: a system inherits controls implemented by another entity — organizational common controls, a hosting provider, or a cloud platform — rather than implementing them itself. The system security plan documents, for each control, whether it is system-specific, common (inherited), or hybrid (partly each). This is essential in modern environments where no system implements every control alone; getting the inheritance mapping right is what makes the shared responsibility explicit and assessable.",
"Cloud makes inheritance central and perilous if misunderstood. A cloud provider implements and is responsible for certain controls (physical security, hypervisor), while the customer remains responsible for others (data, access configuration, application security) — the shared responsibility model (detailed in IC-201). Misunderstanding the boundary, assuming the provider covers a control the customer actually owns, is a leading cause of cloud breaches. RMF inheritance documentation makes this boundary explicit: every control is assigned to a responsible party, so nothing falls through the gap between provider and customer."],
ex:"A system deployed on a FedRAMP-authorized cloud inherits the platform's physical and environmental controls (documented as common/inherited from the provider) while retaining responsibility for its data encryption and access configuration (system-specific). The inheritance mapping in the SSP makes explicit which controls the provider covers and which the customer owns — preventing the assumption-gap that causes cloud misconfigurations.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-53B, overlays","How overlays package tailoring for specific contexts."],["FedRAMP and cloud shared-responsibility documentation","Control inheritance and the provider/customer responsibility split."]],
kc:[
{q:"An overlay is:",o:["A network diagram","A pre-built set of tailoring decisions for a specific community, technology, or environment, applied atop the baseline","A type of firewall","An assessment report"],a:1,x:"Overlays encode expert-consensus tailoring for a common context (privacy, cloud, NSS), avoiding per-team reinvention."},
{q:"Control inheritance documentation is essential in cloud environments because:",o:["Clouds have no controls","It makes the provider/customer responsibility split explicit, preventing controls from falling through the gap","It speeds up the network","Inheritance is illegal in cloud"],a:1,x:"Cloud shared responsibility means some controls are the provider's and some the customer's; explicit inheritance mapping prevents assumption gaps."},
{q:"A leading cause of cloud breaches related to inheritance is:",o:["Too much encryption","Assuming the provider covers a control the customer actually owns (e.g., data/access configuration)","Provider physical security","Overlays being applied"],a:1,x:"Misunderstanding the responsibility boundary leaves customer-owned controls (like access configuration) unimplemented — a common breach cause."}]},
{t:"The System Security Plan",d:"The authoritative record of the system and its controls.",
obj:["Explain the purpose and contents of the SSP","Describe how the SSP documents controls and their implementation","Explain why SSP accuracy is critical to authorization"],
read:["The System Security Plan is the authoritative document describing the system, its boundary, its categorization, and how each selected control is implemented. It is the central artifact of the RMF package: the Authorizing Official relies on it to understand what they are authorizing, and the assessor uses it to know what to test. A good SSP states, for every applicable control, how the system satisfies it — the specific mechanism, configuration, or procedure — not a restatement of the control's requirement.",
"The SSP integrates the outputs of the earlier steps: the system description and boundary from Prepare, the categorization and its rationale from Categorize, and the selected, tailored controls with their inheritance and parameter decisions from Select. As the system is implemented, the SSP records how each control is actually satisfied. This makes the SSP a living document — it must be updated as the system changes, because an SSP that describes a system as it was designed rather than as it is built is precisely the paper-mirroring failure BC-101 warned against.",
"SSP accuracy is not clerical nicety; it is the integrity of the authorization. The AO accepts risk based on the SSP's account of the system and its controls, so an inaccurate SSP means the risk decision rests on false information — and inspectors comparing the SSP to the as-built system find every discrepancy. Well-written implementation statements (the how, in present tense, with named mechanisms and verifiers) are the difference between an SSP that survives assessment and one that generates findings, which is exactly the skill BC-106 develops in depth."],
ex:"An assessor reads an SSP control statement claiming multi-factor authentication is enforced for all administrative access, then verifies it against the system and finds MFA is configured and working. The specific, accurate, verifiable statement passed assessment. A vaguer statement ('the system uses strong authentication') or an inaccurate one (claiming MFA that was never enabled) would have generated a finding — the SSP's accuracy directly determined the outcome.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-18","Guide for developing system security plans."],["NIST SP 800-37 Rev.2","The SSP's role across the RMF steps and in authorization."]],
kc:[
{q:"A good SSP control implementation statement describes:",o:["The control's requirement restated","How the system specifically satisfies the control — the mechanism, configuration, or procedure","Only the vendor name","The project budget"],a:1,x:"The SSP must say what this system does to meet the control, not restate the requirement — that specificity is what the assessor verifies."},
{q:"Why is SSP accuracy critical to authorization?",o:["It affects printing costs","The AO accepts risk based on the SSP; an inaccurate SSP means the risk decision rests on false information","It is not important","It only matters for training"],a:1,x:"The authorization is only as sound as the SSP's account of the system; inaccuracy invalidates the basis of the risk decision."},
{q:"An SSP that describes the system as designed rather than as built is an example of:",o:["Best practice","Paper mirroring — documentation diverging from reality, which inspectors and assessors detect","Proper tailoring","Continuous monitoring"],a:1,x:"A living SSP must track the as-built system; a stale one is the paper-mirroring failure that generates findings and undermines trust."}]},
{t:"RMF Roles and Responsibilities",d:"Who does what across the framework.",
obj:["Identify the key RMF roles and their responsibilities","Explain the separation between implementation and assessment","Explain accountability for the authorization decision"],
read:["The RMF assigns specific roles, and knowing them is basic professional fluency. The Authorizing Official (AO) is the senior official accountable for the risk decision — they grant or deny the ATO and personally own the accepted residual risk. The Information System Owner is responsible for the overall system. The ISSM manages the security program for the system or organization; the ISSO executes day-to-day security (implementation evidence, POA&M maintenance, monitoring). The Security Control Assessor (SCA) independently evaluates the controls. The Risk Executive provides enterprise consistency (module five).",
"A structural principle runs through these roles: separation between those who implement controls and those who assess them. The SCA must be independent of the implementation team so that the assessment the AO relies on is unbiased — you cannot credibly grade your own work. This independence, emphasized in BC-107, is what gives the assessment (and thus the authorization) its integrity. Similarly, the AO's accountability is non-delegable: they may rely on staff work, but the risk acceptance is theirs.",
"These roles map to the accountability-versus-responsibility distinction from BC-101. The AO is accountable for the risk decision; the ISSO and ISSM are responsible for the security work that informs it; the SCA is responsible for independent evaluation. Clear role assignment prevents the diffuse-accountability failure where everyone touches security but no one owns the decision. When you work an RMF package, knowing who plays each role — and respecting the separations between them — is what keeps the process credible and the authorization defensible."],
ex:"On a system authorization, the ISSO gathers implementation evidence and drafts control statements, the SCA independently tests the controls and writes the assessment report, and the AO — reading both — accepts the residual risk and signs the ATO. The ISSO could not assess their own implementation (no independence), and the AO could not delegate the risk acceptance (non-delegable accountability). Each separation preserves the decision's integrity.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-37 Rev.2, roles appendix","The full set of RMF roles and their responsibilities."],["DoDI 8510.01","DoD-specific role assignments and authorities in RMF."]],
kc:[
{q:"The Authorizing Official (AO) is the individual who:",o:["Implements the controls","Is accountable for and personally accepts the system's residual risk by granting the ATO","Tests the controls independently","Writes the code"],a:1,x:"The AO owns the risk decision; that accountability is non-delegable even though staff perform the supporting work."},
{q:"Why must the Security Control Assessor be independent of the implementation team?",o:["To slow the process","So the assessment the AO relies on is unbiased — you cannot credibly grade your own work","Because implementers lack skill","Independence is optional"],a:1,x:"Assessor independence gives the assessment integrity; the AO's risk decision depends on an unbiased evaluation of the controls."},
{q:"Day-to-day security execution — implementation evidence, POA&M maintenance, monitoring — is primarily the responsibility of the:",o:["Authorizing Official","ISSO (with ISSM oversight)","Security Control Assessor","Risk Executive"],a:1,x:"The ISSO executes daily security work under ISSM oversight; the AO decides, the SCA assesses, the Risk Executive frames enterprise risk."}]},
{t:"Privacy and PII in the RMF",d:"Privacy controls, PIAs, and protecting personal data.",
obj:["Explain the privacy dimension of the RMF and the privacy control baseline","Describe the Privacy Impact Assessment and its role","Explain the relationship between security and privacy controls"],
read:["Systems that handle personally identifiable information carry a privacy obligation alongside the security obligation, and the RMF integrates both. SP 800-53 includes privacy controls and SP 800-53B defines a privacy control baseline applied to systems processing PII; the Senior Agency Official for Privacy has a role parallel to the security roles. Privacy is not a subset of security — a system can be secure yet privacy-violating (collecting more data than needed, retaining it too long, using it beyond its stated purpose) — so the framework treats privacy risk as its own dimension requiring its own controls and rationale.",
"The Privacy Impact Assessment (PIA) is the privacy analog of security categorization: a documented analysis of what PII the system collects, why, how it is used, who it is shared with, how it is protected, and how long it is retained. The PIA surfaces privacy risks — over-collection, purpose creep, inadequate retention limits — and drives the selection of privacy controls to address them. For systems with significant PII, the PIA is a required, scrutinized artifact, and skipping or superficially completing it is a common compliance and public-trust failure.",
"Security and privacy controls overlap but are not identical. Access controls and encryption serve both (protecting PII confidentiality), but privacy adds requirements security alone does not — data minimization (collect only what is needed), purpose limitation (use data only for stated purposes), individual participation (notice and consent), and retention limits. The RMF's integration of privacy means a system handling PII must select and implement both baselines and document both risk analyses, connecting the mission-impact thinking of categorization to the distinct question of how personal data is respected."],
ex:"An HR system's PIA reveals it collects Social Security numbers for a purpose that no longer requires them and retains records indefinitely. These are privacy risks invisible to a purely security lens — the data is encrypted and access-controlled, yet over-collected and over-retained. The PIA drives corrective privacy controls: stop collecting the unnecessary field and set a retention limit, addressing risk that security controls alone would never surface.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-53 Rev.5 privacy controls and SP 800-53B privacy baseline","The integrated privacy control set applied to PII-handling systems."],["OMB Circular A-130 and PIA guidance","Privacy obligations and the Privacy Impact Assessment requirement."]],
kc:[
{q:"Privacy is treated as its own dimension in the RMF because:",o:["It is identical to security","A system can be secure yet privacy-violating — over-collecting, over-retaining, or misusing data — requiring distinct controls","Privacy has no controls","Security covers everything"],a:1,x:"Encryption and access control protect PII confidentiality but do not address minimization, purpose limitation, or retention — distinct privacy concerns."},
{q:"A Privacy Impact Assessment documents:",o:["Network topology","What PII is collected, why, how used, shared, protected, and retained — surfacing privacy risks","Firewall rules","Password policies"],a:1,x:"The PIA is the privacy analog of categorization, analyzing the personal-data lifecycle and driving privacy control selection."},
{q:"Data minimization, a privacy requirement, means:",o:["Encrypting all data","Collecting only the PII actually needed for the stated purpose","Deleting all logs","Sharing data widely"],a:1,x:"Minimization limits collection to what is necessary — a privacy principle security controls alone do not enforce."}]},
{t:"Continuous Monitoring Strategy Foundations",d:"Planning for the Monitor step from the start.",
obj:["Explain why continuous monitoring is planned early","Describe what an ISCM strategy covers","Explain ongoing authorization and the shift from periodic reauthorization"],
read:["Although Monitor is the seventh RMF step, its strategy is planned early — during Prepare — because a system must be built and documented to be monitorable. Information Security Continuous Monitoring (ISCM) is the ongoing awareness of security posture: reassessing controls on a cadence, tracking vulnerabilities and their remediation, analyzing the security impact of changes, and reporting status to the AO. Planning this from the start (which control gets reassessed how often, what metrics feed the AO, how changes are analyzed) is what makes authorization sustainable rather than a periodic scramble.",
"An ISCM strategy defines the cadence and scope of ongoing assessment: not every control is reassessed at once, but a risk-prioritized subset is evaluated continuously so that the whole control set is covered over a defined period, with higher-risk controls checked more often. It defines the metrics reported to leadership, the process for security impact analysis when the system changes (module connecting to the significant-versus-minor change decision), and the automated data feeds (vulnerability scans, configuration compliance) that make continuous assessment feasible at scale rather than purely manual.",
"The payoff is ongoing authorization: rather than an ATO expiring after three years and the whole package being redone, a robust continuous monitoring program maintains the authorization through sustained evidence that controls remain effective. The AO makes ongoing risk decisions informed by current data instead of a stale point-in-time snapshot — the continuous-risk-management philosophy from module one, realized. This is why the framework's opening (Prepare) and closing (Monitor) steps are linked: you plan at the start for the monitoring that will sustain the system for its whole life, the discipline IC-204 and the operational tier put into practice."],
ex:"A system's ISCM strategy, defined during Prepare, schedules high-risk controls for quarterly reassessment, feeds monthly vulnerability and configuration-compliance metrics to the AO, and requires a security impact analysis for any significant change. Three years later, instead of a from-scratch reauthorization, the AO reviews the accumulated monitoring evidence and continues the authorization — the early planning made ongoing authorization possible.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-137","Information Security Continuous Monitoring for federal systems."],["NIST SP 800-37 Rev.2, Monitor step and ongoing authorization","How continuous monitoring sustains authorization over time."]],
kc:[
{q:"Why is the continuous monitoring strategy planned during Prepare, not just at the Monitor step?",o:["It is not planned early","A system must be built and documented to be monitorable; planning cadence and metrics early makes authorization sustainable","Monitoring is optional","It replaces categorization"],a:1,x:"Monitorability is designed in; deciding what to reassess, how often, and what metrics feed the AO must be planned before the system is built."},
{q:"An ISCM strategy reassesses controls by:",o:["Checking all controls at once annually","Evaluating a risk-prioritized subset continuously so the full set is covered over a defined period, higher-risk more often","Never reassessing","Only after a breach"],a:1,x:"Continuous monitoring spreads reassessment by risk over time, with high-risk controls checked more frequently — sustainable coverage."},
{q:"Ongoing authorization means:",o:["The ATO never expires and is never reviewed","The authorization is maintained through sustained monitoring evidence rather than a periodic from-scratch reauthorization","Monitoring is skipped","The AO is removed"],a:1,x:"Robust continuous monitoring lets the AO make ongoing, data-informed risk decisions, sustaining authorization instead of redoing it periodically."}]},
{t:"RMF Foundations Capstone",d:"Categorizing and selecting for a real system, end to end.",
obj:["Execute Prepare, Categorize, and Select coherently for a system","Justify categorization and tailoring decisions defensibly","Recognize how early-step quality determines downstream success"],
read:["This module runs the opening steps for one system to show how they interlock. Scenario: an HR application for a federal agency, handling employee PII and payroll data. Prepare defines the authorization boundary (app, database, supporting services), identifies stakeholders and the mission, and notes inherited enterprise common controls. Categorize inventories the data types (PII, payroll), maps them via SP 800-60, and applies FIPS 199 — confidentiality High (large PII exposure would be severe), integrity Moderate, availability Moderate — yielding a High system by the high-water mark, with each rating documented.",
"Select then pulls the SP 800-53B High baseline plus privacy controls, and tailoring fits it to the system: designating inherited common controls (enterprise boundary, identity), scoping out inapplicable controls with rationale, applying the privacy overlay for the PII, and setting organization-defined parameters per the agency's risk tolerance. The SSP records all of this — boundary, categorization rationale, and each control's implementation and inheritance status. Every decision traces back to the mission and data, and forward to a concrete, defensible control set ready for implementation and assessment.",
"The lesson the capstone drives home is leverage: the quality of Prepare, Categorize, and Select determines whether the whole authorization succeeds. A sloppy boundary, a guessed categorization, or convenience-driven tailoring produces a package that fails assessment or, worse, an authorization resting on a mischaracterized system. Done rigorously, the early steps yield a control set that precisely fits the system's real risk, an SSP that survives scrutiny, and an authorization the AO can defend. The graded deliverable — a categorization report and tailored baseline for the HR application — is exactly this early-step rigor made concrete.",],
ex:"The HR-application team, tempted to categorize the system Moderate to reduce control burden, instead documents that the scale of PII makes a confidentiality breach severe — a High rating. This defensible, mission-traced categorization selects the High baseline. Months later an audit examines the categorization and finds it well-justified, and the authorization holds — the early rigor paid off exactly where a convenient under-categorization would have failed.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-37 Rev.2","The end-to-end RMF process the capstone exercises through Select."],["FIPS 199, SP 800-60, SP 800-53B","The categorization and baseline-selection standards applied together."]],
kc:[
{q:"For an HR system handling large volumes of PII, a defensible confidentiality impact is likely:",o:["Low, because HR is routine","High, because a large PII disclosure would have severe mission and privacy consequences","Not applicable","Moderate by default with no analysis"],a:1,x:"The scale and sensitivity of the PII make a confidentiality breach severe — a High rating traceable to mission impact, not convenience."},
{q:"Why does the quality of Prepare, Categorize, and Select determine the authorization's success?",o:["They are the only steps","They front-load the consequential decisions — boundary, impact, and baseline — that every downstream step depends on","They are optional","They replace the assessment"],a:1,x:"Errors in boundary, categorization, or tailoring propagate through implementation and assessment, so early rigor is decisive."},
{q:"Choosing a lower categorization than the mission warrants, to reduce control burden, is:",o:["Efficient tailoring","An indefensible under-categorization that leaves the system under-protected and fails audit","Required by FIPS 199","The Risk Executive's job"],a:1,x:"Categorization must reflect real mission impact; lowering it for convenience under-protects the system and is a serious finding."}]}
],
cap:[
{q:"SCENARIO: A system owner wants to skip RMF documentation and just deploy good tools. The correct explanation is:",o:["Tools are enough","FISMA legally requires the system be categorized, controlled per NIST standards, assessed, and formally authorized before operating","Documentation is optional","Only the firewall matters"],a:1,x:"The RMF is a legal mandate under FISMA, not optional process — authorization by an accountable official is required to operate."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A system's boundary omits a database it depends on. The consequence is:",o:["Faster authorization with no downside","An unauthorized, unassessed dependency — a gap that undermines the risk decision","Stronger security","Lower categorization automatically"],a:1,x:"Excluded-but-relied-upon components fall outside the controls, assessment, and authorization, creating the undocumented-interconnection problem."},
{q:"SCENARIO: Twelve systems each accept a minor legacy-component risk; individually fine, collectively excessive. The function that should catch this is:",o:["Each system's AO alone","The Risk Executive, providing the enterprise view of aggregate risk against organizational tolerance","The assessor","The vendor"],a:1,x:"Individually acceptable risks can aggregate beyond tolerance; the Risk Executive's enterprise perspective catches what single AOs miss."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A system is Low confidentiality, Moderate integrity, Low availability. Its overall FIPS 199 category is:",o:["Low","Moderate, by the high-water mark","High","Undefined"],a:1,x:"The high-water mark takes the highest objective rating; Moderate integrity makes the system Moderate overall."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A team accepts SP 800-60 provisional impacts without adjustment for their market-moving financial data. This is:",o:["Correct and efficient","A shortcut that mis-categorizes — provisional values require documented context-based adjustment","Required by FIPS 200","The assessor's error"],a:1,x:"SP 800-60 defaults are starting points; mission-specific consequences (market-moving disclosure) warrant a documented upward adjustment."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A Moderate system's baseline includes wireless controls, but it has no wireless capability. The correct action is:",o:["Implement the wireless controls anyway","Tailor them out with a documented rationale that no wireless is present","Re-categorize the system as Low","Ignore the baseline entirely"],a:1,x:"Scoping out genuinely inapplicable controls is valid tailoring — with documented rationale, not silent removal."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A cloud-hosted system's team assumes the provider secures the data and access configuration. Months later, an exposed storage bucket is breached. The failure was:",o:["The provider's fault entirely","Misunderstanding shared responsibility — data/access configuration is the customer's control, per the inheritance mapping","Too much encryption","The categorization"],a:1,x:"Cloud inheritance splits responsibility; assuming the provider covers a customer-owned control (access configuration) leaves it unimplemented — a classic cloud breach."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An SSP claims MFA is enforced for admin access, but the assessor finds it was never enabled. The impact is:",o:["Minor paperwork issue","A finding and a risk decision resting on false information — the SSP's inaccuracy undermines the authorization","No impact","Faster authorization"],a:1,x:"The AO accepts risk based on the SSP; an inaccurate control statement means the authorization rests on a mischaracterization — a serious finding."},
{q:"SCENARIO: On an authorization, the ISSO wants to both implement and assess the controls to save time. The problem is:",o:["Nothing, it is efficient","It violates assessor independence — you cannot credibly grade your own implementation, undermining the AO's basis for decision","It is too slow","Only the AO can implement"],a:1,x:"Separation between implementer and assessor gives the assessment integrity; self-assessment removes the independence the AO relies on."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A team is tempted to categorize a large-PII HR system Moderate to reduce control burden. The defensible choice is:",o:["Moderate, since HR is routine","High for confidentiality (severe PII-disclosure impact), selecting the High baseline — categorization must reflect mission impact","Low, to save money","Whatever the vendor recommends"],a:1,x:"Categorization must trace to real mission impact; the scale of PII makes disclosure severe, and convenience-driven under-categorization is indefensible."}
]};
